1
00:00:21,547 --> 00:00:22,849
Good evening, everyone.

2
00:00:22,868 --> 00:00:24,771
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:27,693
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:27,754 --> 00:00:51,283
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Dasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Dasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha

5
00:00:51,618 --> 00:00:53,259
Sam Buddha, Sam.

6
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:57,963
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:00:59,725 --> 00:01:09,253
So yesterday we met Diganaka, who was a thinker, too much lost in his thought.

8
00:01:09,993 --> 00:01:21,304
And the Buddha got him to become fully liberated eventually by grounding him in the body and in feelings.

9
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:33,358
just to get him off his head and to recognize that the path to liberation is using a different faculty that we call our intuitive intelligence, our intuition.

10
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,385
Now here in Magandiya, we have a different sort.

11
00:01:37,424 --> 00:01:40,810
Magandiya is a hedonist.

12
00:01:40,829 --> 00:01:47,882
And there's a quote from William Blake from his book, Proverbs of Hell.

13
00:01:48,959 --> 00:02:17,633
um that's right that's it proverbs of hell the road of excess leads to the palace of wisdom you never know what is enough until you know what is more than enough and that rings true especially for me personally because uh like i think most people you go through a period in your youth of uh in your younger days of drinking a little too much

14
00:02:17,848 --> 00:02:21,332
And now, of course, we don't touch the drop, of course.

15
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:32,506
But in those days, I remember waking up in the morning, lying on the pavement with my head actually in the gutter.

16
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:37,512
And I struggled up and made my way to a local cemetery, which was just down the road.

17
00:02:37,532 --> 00:02:45,461
I was very fortunate because I lay on top of a grave and considered deeply the ridiculousness of getting absolutely palatic.

18
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:46,842
That's the word we used in those days.

19
00:02:47,768 --> 00:02:51,933
And I made a decision that from then on, I would only get tipsy.

20
00:02:52,313 --> 00:02:52,995
And I kept it.

21
00:02:53,556 --> 00:02:54,757
And life was much better.

22
00:02:55,198 --> 00:02:59,364
So the road of excess can lead to the palace of wisdom.

23
00:03:00,205 --> 00:03:02,167
And here we have Magandiya.

24
00:03:03,229 --> 00:03:12,281
And he meets the Buddha in Kuru country.

25
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:13,742
That's up towards Delhi.

26
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:16,847
That's a long way from Benares,

27
00:03:17,332 --> 00:03:20,177
you know, from the Ganges all the way up to Delhi.

28
00:03:20,878 --> 00:03:29,012
So you can see how far the Buddha would walk, probably take a lift in ox carts and stuff, but generally speaking, walking.

29
00:03:30,715 --> 00:03:42,193
So he's made his way and he's staying in the fire chamber of a Brahmin belonging to the Bharadwaja clan.

30
00:03:43,033 --> 00:04:12,906
and uh he's the brahmin has spread out this um uh grass mat as a bed you see uh now i don't i presume that a um a uh where is it where is it a fire chamber uh would be the warm place in the in the house where you had the fire i presume that's like like the front room or something or the sitting room and uh magandia

31
00:04:13,122 --> 00:04:14,143
He's out walking.

32
00:04:14,182 --> 00:04:14,985
He's a wanderer.

33
00:04:15,004 --> 00:04:26,420
So he's also a summoner, which is odd because you would have thought that somebody who's devoted to a pleasurable life would be a lay person trying to make money and to have a good time.

34
00:04:27,141 --> 00:04:31,305
But he is actually a wanderer, a summoner.

35
00:04:31,326 --> 00:04:36,192
And when he walks in, he says, for whom is this bed of grass made for?

36
00:04:36,814 --> 00:04:44,524
And the Brahmin answers, he said, it seems like the bed of a recluse.

37
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:53,173
And the Brahmin says, oh, yes, it is for Master Gautama, you see.

38
00:04:54,095 --> 00:05:00,341
And he says he's got he has a very good reputation.

39
00:05:00,658 --> 00:05:14,877
He's the blessed one, accomplished, fully enlightened, perfect in true knowledge and conduct, sublime, knower of the world, incomparable leader of persons to be tamed, teacher of gods and humans, enlightened and blessed.

40
00:05:14,918 --> 00:05:16,600
That's whom I prepared this bed for.

41
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:24,810
And Bharadvaja says, well, this is a very sad sight indeed, because this is the bed of a life destroyer.

42
00:05:25,331 --> 00:05:29,096
So he's heard that the Buddha, of course, he's all into restraint.

43
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:31,713
and into relinquishment and renunciation.

44
00:05:32,134 --> 00:05:34,697
So he calls it a destroyer of life.

45
00:05:40,363 --> 00:05:47,732
So anyway, it would seem that the Buddha knows all this because of his divine ear.

46
00:05:47,892 --> 00:05:53,478
So he's able to hear what people are saying when it concerns him, I should think.

47
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,923
And when he finally returns,

48
00:05:57,442 --> 00:06:07,293
He greets the Brahmin, of course, and he says to him, Bharadwaja, did you have any conversation with the wanderer Magandhir about this bed of grass?

49
00:06:08,134 --> 00:06:17,204
And of course, the Brahmin was awestruck and his hair stood on end and replied, we wanted to tell Master Gautama about this very thing, but Master Gautama has anticipated us.

50
00:06:21,309 --> 00:06:25,273
And this discussion was left because Magandhir turns up.

51
00:06:25,944 --> 00:06:30,011
exchanging greetings, courteous and amiable talk.

52
00:06:30,050 --> 00:06:37,961
The Buddha launches into this teaching about, well, reminds him what he's just said.

53
00:06:38,021 --> 00:06:52,982
So this must have come as quite a surprise to Magandiya because he said to him, with reference to me, you said the recluse Gautama is a life ruiner, a life destroyer.

54
00:06:53,569 --> 00:06:57,314
And it was with reference to Master Gautama that I did say this, he said.

55
00:06:58,274 --> 00:06:58,956
And why is that?

56
00:06:58,995 --> 00:07:02,781
Because it's recorded in the scriptures, in what we've learned.

57
00:07:03,721 --> 00:07:10,930
So then the Buddha says, well, the eye delights in form, takes delight in form, rejoices in form.

58
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:15,755
That has been tamed by the Tathagata, guarded, protected and restrained.

59
00:07:16,136 --> 00:07:21,062
And he teaches the Dharma for its restraint, for the restraint of indulgence.

60
00:07:21,278 --> 00:07:22,720
Remember, it's always indulgence.

61
00:07:22,759 --> 00:07:26,184
It's not that you're trying to restrain the process of seeing.

62
00:07:26,963 --> 00:07:29,507
And then he runs through the rest of the senses.

63
00:07:31,108 --> 00:07:35,553
These are just the basic five sensual chords, as they're known.

64
00:07:37,315 --> 00:07:46,005
And it is exactly with reference to this that the recluse Gautama is a destroyer of life.

65
00:07:47,266 --> 00:07:47,947
Why is that?

66
00:07:48,309 --> 00:07:49,529
Because it's recorded.

67
00:07:51,197 --> 00:08:02,668
So now the Buddha tries to get him to think differently by giving a couple of the usual similes or stories.

68
00:08:03,569 --> 00:08:04,891
So what do you think?

69
00:08:05,132 --> 00:08:10,137
Someone formerly enjoyed themselves with forms cognizable by the eye.

70
00:08:10,617 --> 00:08:14,261
So what you can see agreeable, likable, et cetera.

71
00:08:14,281 --> 00:08:19,685
And on a later occasion, having understood the origin, disappearance,

72
00:08:20,019 --> 00:08:49,099
gratification the danger and the escape in the case of what we see he might abandon craving for those things remove fever for him for them and abide without thirst with a mind inwardly at peace so now um just note that the origin disappearance and then is gratification the danger and the escape so what do you think of that do you see

73
00:08:49,331 --> 00:08:53,216
And the answer that Magandir is nothing, Master Gautama.

74
00:08:53,496 --> 00:08:55,099
He didn't think anything of it at all.

75
00:08:55,820 --> 00:09:00,485
And then the Buddha runs through the other four senses.

76
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:02,427
So he says, what do you think of that?

77
00:09:02,869 --> 00:09:03,990
And Magandir says, nothing.

78
00:09:04,211 --> 00:09:05,251
I don't think anything about it.

79
00:09:06,072 --> 00:09:08,855
So Magandir, the Buddha then talks about himself.

80
00:09:08,895 --> 00:09:10,999
He says, well, I lived at home.

81
00:09:11,078 --> 00:09:15,745
I enjoyed myself, provided and endowed with the five cords of sensual pleasure.

82
00:09:17,041 --> 00:09:21,004
likeable, connected with sensual desire and provocative of lust.

83
00:09:22,166 --> 00:09:38,062
And then he says on a later occasion, having understood as they actually are, the origin, the disappearance, the gratification, the danger and the escape in the case of sensual pleasure, I abandoned craving for sensual pleasure.

84
00:09:38,081 --> 00:09:39,763
I removed the fever for them.

85
00:09:40,283 --> 00:09:43,726
I abide without thirst with the mind inwardly at peace.

86
00:09:44,827 --> 00:09:46,710
I see other beings who are not free,

87
00:09:46,976 --> 00:09:57,511
from lust for sensual pleasure, greed for sensual pleasure, devoured by craving for sensual pleasure, burning with fever for sensual pleasure, indulging in sensual pleasure.

88
00:09:58,113 --> 00:10:01,677
And I do not envy them, nor do I delight therein.

89
00:10:01,859 --> 00:10:02,539
Why is that?

90
00:10:03,201 --> 00:10:12,394
Because there is a delight apart from sensual pleasure, apart from unwholesome stage, which surpasses,

91
00:10:13,217 --> 00:10:16,681
this particular, even divine bliss.

92
00:10:17,562 --> 00:10:20,044
So here he's pointing to Nibbana.

93
00:10:20,504 --> 00:10:32,297
So all these sensual pleasures and even the pleasures of meditation through the jhana are surpassed by the bliss of Nibbana.

94
00:10:32,317 --> 00:10:38,482
Since I take delight in that, I do not envy the inferior, nor do I delight in them.

95
00:10:41,365 --> 00:10:42,647
And then he gives an example.

96
00:10:42,726 --> 00:10:50,018
Supposing a householder with great wealth and property provided and endowed with the five chords of sensual pleasure.

97
00:10:51,561 --> 00:11:02,297
And on the dissolution of his body, he ends up in a heavenly world with a retinue of the 33, surrounded by a group of nymphs in the Nandana Grove.

98
00:11:02,576 --> 00:11:07,384
This reminds you of the 72 virgins awaiting jihadi martyrs.

99
00:11:09,067 --> 00:11:10,308
And he would enjoy himself.

100
00:11:10,998 --> 00:11:13,740
with the divine sensual pleasure.

101
00:11:14,842 --> 00:11:18,668
Now, then he asks, what do you think?

102
00:11:18,988 --> 00:11:35,009
Would that young God surrounded by a group of nymphs in the Nandana Grove, enjoying himself, provided and endowed with the five chords of divine sensual pleasure, envy the householder for the five chords of human sensual pleasure?

103
00:11:35,789 --> 00:11:38,472
Would he return to the human sensual pleasures?

104
00:11:38,807 --> 00:11:40,831
I know, Master Gautama.

105
00:11:41,192 --> 00:11:46,399
Mahavendiyya says, of course, that the divine pleasures are much more excellent and sublime.

106
00:11:48,464 --> 00:11:50,967
And then the Buddha talks about himself.

107
00:11:50,988 --> 00:11:52,089
Sorry.

108
00:11:52,649 --> 00:11:53,171
Yes.

109
00:11:53,191 --> 00:11:59,321
And then he talks about himself, that he had formerly lived like this and that he had woken up to the dangers.

110
00:12:00,222 --> 00:12:06,131
Then he gives a simile around a leper.

111
00:12:07,158 --> 00:12:21,750
When I traveled to India for the first time, there were lots of people in Calcutta, in Delhi and whatnot, who were seriously ill with all sorts of illnesses, just lying in the streets.

112
00:12:21,831 --> 00:12:24,634
And some of them were dying, I presume.

113
00:12:25,033 --> 00:12:29,458
But the most pitiable of them was the leper.

114
00:12:30,198 --> 00:12:32,581
Very rarely came across them.

115
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:34,363
When they came, they were always shrouded.

116
00:12:34,933 --> 00:12:47,356
always seemingly enclosed in upon themselves a really really sorry sight i think these days there are colonies for them and of course it's a bacterial disease it can actually be cured

117
00:12:50,071 --> 00:13:05,634
So this gives you an idea of what it was like in those days, the leper with sores and blisters on his limbs being devoured by worms, scratching the scabs off the opening of his wounds with his nails, cauterising his body over the burning charcoal pit.

118
00:13:06,615 --> 00:13:16,129
I believe that it can be very itchy and sometimes by putting your fingers or the part of the limb that's really itching onto something really

119
00:13:16,767 --> 00:13:20,150
you know, that's going to burn it or cauterize it, it takes that away.

120
00:13:20,611 --> 00:13:23,034
But there's a lot of pain in leprosy.

121
00:13:24,537 --> 00:13:30,604
And then his friends and companions would bring a physician to him and the physician would make medicine and cure his leprosy.

122
00:13:31,365 --> 00:13:33,648
And he'd become well, happy and independent.

123
00:13:34,428 --> 00:13:39,475
Then he might see another leper with sores and blisters on his limbs, being devoured, et cetera, et cetera.

124
00:13:40,015 --> 00:13:40,616
What do you think?

125
00:13:40,677 --> 00:13:42,960
Would that man envy that leper?

126
00:13:43,192 --> 00:13:48,841
for his burning charcoal pit or his use of medicine?"

127
00:13:48,861 --> 00:13:57,716
They say, no, no, of course not, because when there is sickness, medicine has to be made, and when there's no sickness, medicine does not have to be made.

128
00:13:57,735 --> 00:14:11,596
So it's a case of when somebody is cured, when the leper is cured of his disease, he obviously does not envy when he sees a leper having to put his

129
00:14:11,864 --> 00:14:19,091
his limbs against charcoal in order to cure the itching.

130
00:14:19,111 --> 00:14:35,789
And then he says, well, again, with the leper, with all these saws scratching at his limbs and all that, then once he's been cured by this medicine, two strong men would seize him by both arms and drag him towards a burning charcoal pit.

131
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:37,471
What do you think, Magandhiya?

132
00:14:37,510 --> 00:14:39,714
Would that man twist his body this way and that?

133
00:14:39,734 --> 00:14:40,875
Would he try to escape?

134
00:14:41,210 --> 00:14:43,131
Well, yes, of course he would, you see.

135
00:14:43,152 --> 00:14:44,494
So what do you think?

136
00:14:45,195 --> 00:14:55,086
Is it only now that the fire is painful to touch, hot and scorching, or previously too was the fire painful to touch, hot and scorching?

137
00:14:56,006 --> 00:14:59,071
So Master Gautama Magandiya replies.

138
00:14:59,091 --> 00:15:09,342
So in other words, what's the difference between the charcoal pit for the man cured of leprosy and the charcoal pit for the man when he had leprosy?

139
00:15:10,572 --> 00:15:13,916
Previously, the fire was painful to touch.

140
00:15:15,778 --> 00:15:21,066
The fire is now painful to touch and previously too, the fire was painful to touch.

141
00:15:21,466 --> 00:15:31,458
But when the man was a leper with sores and blisters being devoured, et cetera, and his faculties impaired, thus the fire was actually painful to touch.

142
00:15:31,759 --> 00:15:37,407
He mistakenly perceived it as pleasant because of course it was taking away a greater pain.

143
00:15:39,664 --> 00:15:57,402
So, and then he goes on, he says, still with the leper, so we're still with the leper who's devoured by worms, scratching the scabs off his open stores.

144
00:15:57,542 --> 00:16:09,333
The fouler and more evil smelling and more infected the opening of his wounds would become, yet he would find a certain measure of satisfaction and enjoyment in scratching the opening of his wounds.

145
00:16:10,275 --> 00:16:33,549
yet they would find a certain measure of satisfaction enjoying dependence on the five chords of sensual pleasure so so too magandia beings are not free from lust of sensual pleasure who are devoured by craving so just as the um as the leper is overcome by all this suffering and has to

146
00:16:33,934 --> 00:16:39,908
has to create a greater suffering to get rid of the present suffering through the charcoal.

147
00:16:40,269 --> 00:16:46,722
So the problem with our indulgence is that it bites back.

148
00:16:47,003 --> 00:16:52,315
So remember, when we're addicted to something, when we are dependent on something,

149
00:16:52,514 --> 00:16:55,980
the action becomes compulsive, the habit overtakes us.

150
00:16:56,701 --> 00:17:06,056
And then the consequences of that, of course, are grief when you lose what you're dependent on, frustration when you can't get what you want.

151
00:17:06,096 --> 00:17:11,265
There is, of course, anxiety there that you might lose it anyway.

152
00:17:11,244 --> 00:17:23,045
And then there always comes that point with something that we're enjoying where it becomes boring, where it loses that oomph, it loses that buzz, it loses that level of excitement.

153
00:17:23,605 --> 00:17:27,251
And the boredom then makes us chase for the next excitement.

154
00:17:27,752 --> 00:17:33,742
So there's the pain, there's the dissatisfaction within the body.

155
00:17:34,059 --> 00:17:52,743
within the central pleasure and that's the danger right so there's the gratification the danger of it is the future suffering and the escape of course is renunciation uh has

156
00:17:57,787 --> 00:18:00,411
So he's here now talking about a king.

157
00:18:00,912 --> 00:18:19,818
So, I too, I've never seen or heard a king enjoying himself, provided and endowed with the five calls of pleasure without abandoning sensual pleasure, without removing the fever of sensual pleasure, was able to abide free of thirst with a mind inwardly at peace.

158
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:23,845
And who is able and who will be able to so abide?

159
00:18:23,825 --> 00:18:30,336
So here the king who has all the pleasures of his life still doesn't have this inner peace.

160
00:18:31,739 --> 00:18:40,855
But these recluses and Brahmins who abide, who abided and abide and will abide free of thirst with a mind inwardly at peace,

161
00:18:41,122 --> 00:19:09,946
all do so after having understood as they actually are the origin the disappearance so pleasure there's always a moment where the pleasure begins and the moment it ends the sense of gratification but then the danger the consequence of attachment and the escape in in the case of sensual pleasure and after abandoning craving for sensual pleasure removing the fever for sensual pleasures they abided and abide and will abide

162
00:19:10,551 --> 00:19:14,135
free from thirst with a mind inwardly at peace.

163
00:19:14,955 --> 00:19:24,165
So here he's really trying to get Bhagavad Gita to understand how important it is to let go of his idea that sensual pleasures are nirvana.

164
00:19:24,826 --> 00:19:26,228
And this is what he says, you see.

165
00:19:27,288 --> 00:19:36,238
He asks him, well, he now utters this verse, the greatest of all gains is health,

166
00:19:36,521 --> 00:19:38,490
Nibbana is the greatest bliss.

167
00:19:38,971 --> 00:19:44,253
The Eightfold Path is the best of paths, for it leads safely to the deathless.

168
00:19:44,722 --> 00:19:46,964
And Mahagandir repeats the first two lines.

169
00:19:47,005 --> 00:19:48,787
The greatest of all gains is health.

170
00:19:49,207 --> 00:19:51,008
Nibbana is the greatest bliss.

171
00:19:51,469 --> 00:20:02,018
So he's not quite got it because he says, when the Buddha says, now, what do you mean by health?

172
00:20:02,077 --> 00:20:03,459
What do you mean by Nibbana?

173
00:20:04,259 --> 00:20:09,304
Mahagandir rubbed the limbs with his hands and said, this is that health.

174
00:20:09,324 --> 00:20:13,448
In other words, this body, Master Gautama, and this is that Nibbana.

175
00:20:13,681 --> 00:20:16,344
So I am now happy and healthy.

176
00:20:16,864 --> 00:20:34,182
So often when you, if you say to somebody that, you know, Buddhism is about the end of suffering, unsatisfactoriness, and they say, you know, well, well, I'm happy, you know, I mean, I'm content with my life and I'm happy and I don't see a problem.

177
00:20:34,961 --> 00:20:42,490
So they, these people are hedonists in the sense that they think that the only happiness you can get in life

178
00:20:42,757 --> 00:20:44,660
is based upon your sensual pleasures.

179
00:20:47,344 --> 00:20:49,227
So now he gives him another simile.

180
00:20:49,247 --> 00:21:06,973
He says, what if a man is born blind and he heard a man with good eyesight say, good indeed, sirs, is a white cloth, beautiful, spotless and clean.

181
00:21:07,734 --> 00:21:11,160
And when he heard that, this blind man, he went in search of one.

182
00:21:11,663 --> 00:21:14,467
And he accepted a cloth from somebody.

183
00:21:16,809 --> 00:21:19,814
Good indeed is a white cloth, beautiful, spotless and clean.

184
00:21:20,494 --> 00:21:24,819
So he accepted, but he was given, he was cheated by this man.

185
00:21:25,141 --> 00:21:30,587
He was given a dirty soiled garment and he put it on and he was perfectly satisfied with it.

186
00:21:31,568 --> 00:21:37,636
Because it wasn't that he was knowing or seeing, but out of faith in the man with good eyesight.

187
00:21:37,656 --> 00:21:39,138
He didn't think he was being cheated.

188
00:21:40,351 --> 00:21:45,017
So too, Magandhiya, other sects are blind and visionless.

189
00:21:45,458 --> 00:21:46,419
They are visionless.

190
00:21:47,059 --> 00:21:48,402
They do not know health.

191
00:21:48,461 --> 00:21:49,884
They do not see Nirvana.

192
00:21:50,244 --> 00:21:54,130
So in other words, they didn't add the other two verses.

193
00:21:54,810 --> 00:21:56,574
The greatest of all gains is health.

194
00:21:56,634 --> 00:21:58,455
Nirvana is the greatest bliss.

195
00:21:58,596 --> 00:22:04,203
But the greatest of all, the Eightfold Path is the best of paths.

196
00:22:04,724 --> 00:22:09,852
And it leads safely to the deathless, which is another way of saying

197
00:22:10,102 --> 00:22:39,416
nirvana and then he points out to magandiya this body is a disease a tumor a dart a calamity an affliction referring to this body to say that that this is that health this is that nirvana you do not have the noble vision magandiya by means of which you might know what real health is and what and what is nirvana now at this point it seems um

